12.text:
J – Impossible to tell from the given statement on a biological realism reading.

C is nectar caffeine concentration. The mediation C→a→F→b→Y, C→c→R→d→Y, C→e→Y is the classic nectar methylxanthine pollinator-manipulation model developed for caffeine-producing systems such as Coffea, Camellia etc.

Citrus × sinensis and Rutaceae in general do not synthesize caffeine for nectar. In wild type oranges C≈0 and a transgenic “knock-out of caffeine production’’ therefore does not create a contrast in the exogenous variable C in this genus. With no induced variation in C the a,b,c,d,e path coefficients are unidentified in an orange background; the experiment as stated cannot estimate the signs of the caffeine→foraging/retention→yield mediation.

That is the external-validity objection. If the orange system is taken as a purely hypothetical caffeine-producing background and the knock-out is allowed to actually change C, the pollinator-manipulation literature gives the stimulant/fidelity/cost story that is repeatedly invoked:

* C↑ stimulates/arouses visitors so flower-level residence time per flower falls, a < 0;
* a longer F is a handling-time cost for the pollinator, i.e. F↑ → flowers serviced per unit time ↓ → Y↓, b < 0, so a·b >0;
* C↑ is a learned reward/memory cue and increases patch/plant fidelity/return, c >0;
* greater retention/constancy increases effective pollination per plant, d >0, so c·d >0;
* alkaloid biosynthesis is a resource/metabolic sink with no direct productive role in fruit, e <0.

That is the a -, b -, c +, d +, e - pattern, option B, which is the sign set most colleagues are converging on for a caffeine-producing plant. It remains a hypothetical sign pattern for oranges because the genus does not present the C variation the model requires.

